---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'releasenotes' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Release notes — Toolkit 1.x"
  description="What changed for you, release by release, written for the person who uses it.">
<div class="docs rn">
      <aside class="rn-side">
        <p class="grp">release line:</p>
        <div class="rn-line">
          <span class="rn-ln">Toolkit 1.x</span>
          <span class="rn-lv">1.0.0-rc.2</span>
          <span class="rn-ld">November 12, 2026 · 23 releases</span>
        </div>
        <p class="grp">on this page:</p>
        <ul class="rn-jump">
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-rc-2" class="cur">1.0.0-rc.2</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-rc-1">1.0.0-rc.1</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-21">1.0.0-beta.21</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-20">1.0.0-beta.20</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-19">1.0.0-beta.19</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-18">1.0.0-beta.18</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-17">1.0.0-beta.17</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-16">1.0.0-beta.16</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-15">1.0.0-beta.15</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-14">1.0.0-beta.14</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-13">1.0.0-beta.13</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-12">1.0.0-beta.12</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-11">1.0.0-beta.11</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-10">1.0.0-beta.10</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-9">1.0.0-beta.9</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-8">1.0.0-beta.8</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-7">1.0.0-beta.7</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-6">1.0.0-beta.6</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-5">1.0.0-beta.5</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-4">1.0.0-beta.4</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-3">1.0.0-beta.3</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-2">1.0.0-beta.2</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-1">1.0.0-beta.1</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="grp">also:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={'https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md'} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Full changelog ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href={href('docsindex', 'en')}>Documentation</a></li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <div class="dmain">
        <div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>release notes</b> / toolkit 1.x</p>
          <h1>Release notes</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">A release-by-release account of what actually changes when you use the toolkit: new capabilities, fixes, and behaviour you may notice. For the exhaustive technical record — including constraints, compatibility details, and edge cases — see the changelog.</p>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">one thing to keep in mind</span>
            <p style="margin:0">The extension starts in <code>assist</code> mode. By default, it helps you write compliant comments and points out problems without preventing you from submitting them. Stricter behaviour is something your organization explicitly chooses to enable. A comment is only checked in the browser of someone who has the extension installed, and the extension never blocks a merge.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="rn-major">Version 1</h2>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-rc-2">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-rc.2</span><span class="rel-m">rc</span><span class="rel-d">November 12, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-rc.2" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Two fixes to the thread filter on GitHub's new Overview page.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>On GitHub's Overview, the thread filter is no longer placed inside a thread.</strong> When the Comments section showed <code>Unresolved</code> or <code>Mine</code>, the filter's chips appeared in the middle of the first thread, between its code and its comment. They now sit above the first thread, as in <code>All</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>On GitHub's Overview, filtering by label hides the whole thread.</strong> A thread that did not match the chosen label lost its comments but kept its file name and code on screen, as an empty box. It now disappears entirely, and comes back when you choose <code>all</code>.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The reviewer skill installs Slugger under its new name.</strong> The tool that <code>skills/review-pull-request</code> uses to name review sessions is now published as <code>Slugger</code> instead of <code>Slugger.Cli</code>; the command is still <code>slugger</code>. An installation made under the old name keeps working, and the skill's setup step says how to move to the new one.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-rc-1">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-rc.1</span><span class="rel-m">rc</span><span class="rel-d">November 11, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-rc.1" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The release candidate for 1.0.0: GitLab support, GitHub's new Overview page, a working Azure DevOps, review-session markers and a <code>summary</code> label; <code>enforce</code> now really blocks, after re-reading the configuration, and the verifier is retired for good.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>GitLab (gitlab.com) is supported, without blocking.</strong> Merge requests on gitlab.com get the toolbar, the badges, the thread filter and the banner. Enable gitlab.com from the settings page, like any other site. The extension cannot read a repository's configuration on GitLab yet, so it never blocks a comment there. GitLab Self-Managed and Dedicated are not supported yet.</li>
            <li><strong>GitHub's new Overview page is supported.</strong> On the Overview, the extension did not recognise the new comment field, so nothing was blocked in <code>enforce</code> mode, and many comments got no badges. Both the Overview and the classic Conversation page now work. The banner sits under the pull request header, and the thread filter above the list of threads.</li>
            <li><strong>Review-session markers.</strong> A line <code>[session:&lt;id&gt;]</code> in a comment says which review session wrote it, so that the comments of several agents or several runs can be told apart. The extension hides the line and shows a discreet <code>🤖 session: &lt;id&gt;</code> at the bottom of the comment; the marker never affects validation. Add a description after an em dash, <code>[session:&lt;id&gt; — <em>&lt;sentence&gt;</em>]</code>, and it appears when you hover the mention. Two markers in one comment show <code>#ERROR</code> and a warning that never blocks.</li>
            <li><strong>A <code>summary</code> label to recap a review pass.</strong> <code>summary</code> (📋, typed as <code>?sum</code>) says what was checked and what is still open; it raises no point and answers none. It sits with <code>reviewed</code> in a new group of labels that say where a review stands, at the end of the toolbar's first row. A repository can add its own label to that group with <code>"kind": "status"</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Problems in your configuration are shown, in your language.</strong> When a key is unknown or retired, when the configuration is invalid, or when your organization's policy overrides a setting, the extension used to say nothing. It now lists these notices in the status pill and on the settings page, in English or French, naming where the problem comes from: your organization's policy, the organization's document or the repository's file. They inform; they never change whether a comment is accepted.</li>
            <li><strong>The journal records comment fields the extension could not fully understand.</strong> A field whose send buttons were not found, or that the extension placed by default without recognising it, now leaves a line in the settings page's journal, so a page the extension misreads can be reported.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>In <code>enforce</code> mode, non-compliant comments are now blocked on every pull request.</strong> Until now, <code>enforce</code> only blocked pull requests created after an activation date that was not set by default, so most installations in <code>enforce</code> never blocked anything. That condition is gone: if your repositories use <code>enforce</code>, expect comments to be blocked after this update. Before blocking, the extension re-reads the configuration, so a repository just switched back to <code>assist</code> is not blocked: the button is released and you are asked to send again.</li>
            <li><strong>The verifier is retired, and its settings with it.</strong> The product reports the unresolved blocking threads of a pull request; it never blocks a merge, and no longer greys the merge button. The keys <code>formatSeverity</code>, <code>overrideLabel</code>, <code>resolverOverrideGroup</code>, <code>activation</code> and <code>docUrl</code> are no longer read, and are shown as unknown keys. The mode <code>warn</code> works as <code>assist</code>, with a notice saying the value is obsolete. If your configuration uses any of these, you can remove them.</li>
            <li><strong>The banner is collapsed by default and shows the count of unresolved blocking threads alone.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Azure DevOps is supported on <code>dev.azure.com</code> only.</strong> Organizations on <code>&#123;org&#125;.visualstudio.com</code> or on Azure DevOps Server are not covered.</li>
            <li><strong>If your configuration already defines a <code>summary</code> label, it needs one property to keep working as before.</strong> Your label now merges into the shipped one and becomes always non-blocking, so <code>summary (blocking):</code> is refused. Add <code>"alwaysNonBlocking": false</code> to restore it.</li>
            <li><strong>Telemetry label counts say what kind of label was used.</strong> If your organization collects telemetry, each label count now carries its kind — <code>remark</code>, <code>status</code> or <code>reply</code> — so that replies and recaps can be counted apart. Nothing else is added.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>Azure DevOps works.</strong> The extension had never worked on Azure DevOps; it is rebuilt from real pages. Comment fields, send buttons and threads are recognised, the live preview gets its badges, the toolbar follows the Azure DevOps theme, a blocked button looks disabled, and the repository's configuration is read, so <code>enforce</code> applies there.</li>
            <li><strong>Keyboard shortcuts no longer get around <code>enforce</code>.</strong> On Azure DevOps, pressing Enter, Ctrl+Enter or Space on the Comment button published a non-compliant comment. These keys are now blocked like a click.</li>
            <li><strong>On GitHub, “Close with comment” and the Overview's Comment button no longer get around <code>enforce</code>.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>A comment that quotes code or text before its label gets its badge.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>A repository can ask for a shorter configuration cache than your organization's policy.</strong> The policy's cache duration was imposed as is, so a repository wanting fresher settings was forced back to it. It is now a maximum, as intended.</li>
            <li><strong>On Azure DevOps, editing an existing comment is no longer mistaken for writing a new one.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>The diagnostic journal keeps the evidence of a problem that never went away,</strong> instead of replacing it with a later observation.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-21">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.21</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">November 2, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.21" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The extension no longer goes silent on GitHub after you restart your browser.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The extension stays active after the browser restarts.</strong> After a browser restart, the extension could do nothing at all on GitHub — no badges, no banner, no toolbar, and no blocking even in <code>enforce</code> mode — until you opened its settings page. The browser was dropping the extension's script at restart, and nothing set it up again. The script now survives a restart, and the extension also sets it up again as soon as the browser starts. If you had to open the settings page after each restart to get the extension back, you no longer need to. The diagnostic archive now shows that start-up step too.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-20">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.20</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">November 1, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.20" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The diagnostic archive now shows how the extension was activated in your browser, to track down pages where it does nothing at all; two settings fields are easier to read.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The diagnostic archive records how the extension's script was set up in your browser.</strong> On some GitHub pages the extension can be completely inactive — no badges, no toolbar, and nothing blocked even in <code>enforce</code> mode — until you open its settings page. Looking into it from outside repairs it, so the cause could not be seen. The extension now keeps a short history of each time it set up, updated or removed its script: what triggered it, which sites it covered, what the browser reported before and after, and any error. If the problem happens to you, download the diagnostic from the settings page before anything else and attach it to an issue; the download is offered even when the journal has no other line. The history holds no page address, and “Clear the log” clears it too.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The settings page's journal no longer shows the legend about page marks.</strong> The marks and their links work as before.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The hints inside the “Direct shortcuts” and “Button order” fields are readable in both themes.</strong> They are quieter than what you type, but no longer too faint to read. The hint of the “Domain to allow” field is unchanged.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-19">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.19</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 31, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.19" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">When a part of the page is not recognised, the journal now records what the page showed at that moment, and the whole diagnostic downloads as an archive to attach to a bug report; editing a comment on GitHub no longer writes a false alarm.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The journal records what the page showed when something was not recognised.</strong> A line now says how far the extension got in the page, what it found there instead and when it happened; for the merge button and the PR creation date, it also says whether the element showed up a moment later. That is often enough for a developer to find the cause without asking you to capture the page. It keeps the page's structure and interface attributes only, never the page's text: not what you type, not comments or code. Attribute values are kept, though, and an interface label such as a button's accessible name, or an account name, can appear in them. It stays on your device.</li>
            <li><strong>A skeleton of the page is kept from the moment of the problem, when your browser and storage allow it.</strong> It is the page's structure, without the page's text, taken at the instant something failed rather than afterwards. Skeletons take at most 1 MB of the extension's storage, the oldest go first, and the extension never lets them crowd out anything else. When the browser cannot compress a skeleton or storage is short, none is stored: the journal line still names it, and the archive simply lacks that file.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>“Copy the whole log” becomes “Download the diagnostic (.zip)”.</strong> The archive holds the journal, the skeletons still stored and a README that says what each file contains; attach it to an issue. Copying a single line works as before, and “Clear the log” now clears the skeletons too. The archive holds the addresses of the pages involved, which on an enterprise server name a repository and a review: read the archive before you share it, keeping in mind that an interface label can name an account.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>Editing a comment on GitHub no longer writes a false alarm to the journal.</strong> A hidden field of GitHub's “Reference in new issue” dialog, which is not a comment field, was reported as a comment field not recognised although nothing was wrong.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-18">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.18</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 30, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.18" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The settings page's journal says what went wrong in a sentence, records the extension's own errors and can be copied for a bug report; filtering threads by label works on GitHub again.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The journal on the settings page can be copied for a bug report.</strong> Each line has a copy icon, and a button copies the whole journal. What you paste into an issue carries the extension version, your browser and what a developer needs to reproduce the problem. Page content is not collected as a field of its own, but the address of the page is in the report, and the message and details of an error can contain snippets of what the extension was handling. Read the whole report, not only the address, before you paste it into a public issue.</li>
            <li><strong>Each journal line says what went wrong in a sentence.</strong> “Merge button not found: it cannot be greyed out” instead of <code>merge-button</code>, with a level, <code>WARN</code> or <code>ERROR</code>. The technical name stays in the copy.</li>
            <li><strong>The extension now records its own errors.</strong> A crash used to leave no trace. It now shows up as an <code>ERROR</code> line that says what the extension was doing, and the details are in the copy. They stay on your device.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>Filtering threads by label hides them on GitHub again.</strong> GitHub forced every thread to stay visible, so a thread you had filtered out stayed on screen. Selecting <code>praise</code> no longer leaves an <code>issue</code> thread showing.</li>
            <li><strong>Clearing the filter gives a thread its original display back.</strong> A thread that already had a forced display setting lost it once the filter was cleared, and GitHub's own styling could override it again.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-17">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.17</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 29, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.17" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Two fields of the settings page no longer show up white in the dark theme.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The “Button order” field and the configuration block of the added labels now follow the dark theme.</strong> Both stayed white on a dark settings page, which made them hard to read and out of place next to the other fields. They now look like the rest of the page.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-16">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.16</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 29, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.16" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The settings page tells you which version you are running, and the GitHub adapter reads a pull request's merge button and resolved conversations correctly again.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The settings page shows the extension's version next to its title.</strong> A release candidate reads <code>v1.0.0-beta.16</code>, where <code>chrome://extensions</code> shows <code>1.0.0</code> for every beta, so you can tell which build you are running before you report a problem. Firefox shows its own version number.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The banner no longer counts resolved conversations as unresolved.</strong> On a pull request whose resolved conversations GitHub collapses, the banner listed them among the unresolved ones, <code>issue (blocking)</code> threads included. It now agrees with the Files changed view of the same pull request.</li>
            <li><strong>The extension finds the merge button of an open pull request on github.com again.</strong> GitHub changed how the button is built and the extension no longer recognised it. Nothing changes on screen yet: the extension greys the button only when a verifier publishes a failing state, and none ships.</li>
            <li><strong>The settings page no longer reports false selector failures.</strong> Its Status section listed searches that failed because there was nothing to find, such as the merge button of a merged pull request. They are no longer logged.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-15">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.15</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 29, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.15" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">A label's badge now tells the reader what a comment means, and its button tells the writer when to use it.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The badge and the button no longer show the same tooltip.</strong> Hovering the label badge of a published comment tells you what the comment means, without an example: <code>issue</code> reads “A problem spotted in this code; the comment explains which one.” Hovering the label's toolbar button tells you when to use it, then gives an example: “Flag a problem to fix, ideally with a lead.” Every shipped label has both texts, in English and French. A badge text never claims more than the label says: whether a point blocks is still shown by the decoration badge next to it.</li>
            <li><strong>The reviewer skill shows priorities as coloured badges.</strong> An agent following <code>skills/review-pull-request</code> now marks each finding with a P0 to P3 badge; where the image does not show, the reader sees <code>[P1]</code> as before.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A team can write each text: <code>labels[].usage</code> for the button.</strong> In <code>.conventional-comments.json</code>, <code>description</code> now goes to the badge and <code>usage</code> to the button. Written, each replaces the shipped text; setting it to an empty string (<code>""</code>) removes that tooltip. Without <code>usage</code>, the button shows <code>description</code>, so a configuration written for 1.0.0-beta.14 keeps showing the same text on both.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-14">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.14</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 29, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.14" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Labels explain themselves where you read them: a hover on a badge shows its definition, and a team can write its own. <code>quibble</code> steps aside for <code>nitpick</code>, and two skills let AI agents review the toolkit's way.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A badge tells you what its label means.</strong> Hover the label badge of a published comment and you get the same tooltip as its toolbar button: the definition and an example, in the interface language. The badge also keeps the arrow cursor rather than the text-selection bar.</li>
            <li><strong>Your team can define its labels: <code>labels[].description</code>.</strong> Write a description for a label you create, or for a shipped one such as <code>issue</code>, in <code>.conventional-comments.json</code>, and the tooltip of the button and the badge shows it. Written, it replaces the shipped definition and its example. Setting it to an empty string (<code>""</code>) removes the tooltip. Omitting it preserves the built-in label's default definition. It is shown as written, whatever the interface language.</li>
            <li><strong>Two review skills for AI agents.</strong> <code>skills/review-pull-request</code> and <code>skills/respond-to-review</code> tell an agent how to review a pull request, or answer its review, with this toolkit's labels: findings followed up until verified, <code>reviewed:</code> once nothing is left, reply labels for the author, and never an approval or a merge. See the <a href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/blob/main/README.md#review-skills-for-ai-agents" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">README</a>.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong><code>quibble</code> is off by default.</strong> Conventional Comments itself describes it as “very much like nitpick”, so the toolbar showed two buttons for one meaning. Use <code>nitpick</code>, or bring <code>quibble</code> back with <code>&#123; "id": "quibble", "enabled": true &#125;</code> in <code>.conventional-comments.json</code>. Until you do, a <code>quibble:</code> comment is an unknown label where comments are validated.</li>
            <li><strong><code>reviewed</code> now says when to use it.</strong> Its tooltip read “you have finished reading”; it now reads “your points are dealt with, you have nothing to add”. Post it once each of your points is fixed and checked, or set aside by a reply you accept. Any later change reopens your review. It still approves nothing.</li>
            <li><strong>The extension's description no longer mentions the companion server</strong>, removed in 1.0.0-beta.13, in <code>chrome://extensions</code> and in the Release pages.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A custom label no longer shows its internal key as a tooltip.</strong> Hovering the button of a label your repository created, such as <code>risk</code>, showed “label.risk”. It now shows its <code>description</code> if it has one, and nothing otherwise.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-13">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.13</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">October 29, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.13" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Labels for replying to a review, every shipped label enabled by default, GitHub's comment box recognised again on today's pages — and the companion server retired, with no verifier in its place yet.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>Labels for replying to a review.</strong> <code>thanks</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>ack</code> and <code>disagree</code> answer a point; <code>reviewed</code> closes your own review and approves nothing. They are the toolkit's own addition, not part of Conventional Comments, and they never block. <code>fixed</code> is accepted for <code>done</code>, <code>acknowledged</code> for <code>ack</code> and <code>thx</code> for <code>thanks</code>; <code>?thx</code> expands to <code>thanks: </code> and <code>?r</code> to <code>reviewed: </code>. They do not make replies require a label. A repository that wants to stick to Conventional Comments disables each of them with one <code>labels[]</code> entry: the options page shows that block and copies it for you.</li>
            <li><strong>The toolbar puts the replies on a line of their own.</strong> Labels that raise a point come first; under a thin rule follow those that answer one — <code>thanks</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>ack</code>, <code>decision</code>, <code>disagree</code>, from the warmest to the firmest. A thin double rule separates the labels from the decorations.</li>
            <li><strong>You choose the order of the label buttons.</strong> “Button order”, in the options, takes a comma-separated list: the labels you list come first, the others follow in the default order, and the completion list follows the same order. It is a personal preference.</li>
            <li><strong>A label can say whether it raises a point or answers one</strong>, with <code>labels[].kind</code> (<code>"remark"</code> or <code>"reply"</code>). A label you declare is <code>"remark"</code> unless you say otherwise. It changes no verdict.</li>
            <li><strong>Every line of the journal says which page it came from.</strong> It recorded which selector failed and when, never where: faced with four lines stamped within the same second, nothing told you whether they came from one page or four. Each line now starts with a six-character mark — same mark, same page — and clicking it opens the exact recorded address in a new tab. That address stays on your device: when you have allowed telemetry, it still sends nothing but the selector's name. Lines already in the journal before this update have no address — it was not being recorded — and carry a neutral mark until their selector fails again, or until you clear the journal.</li>
            <li><strong>The journal clears from the options page.</strong> A button empties it; tabs you already have open start measuring again straight away, and the section updates itself when one of them writes, without a reload.</li>
            <li><strong>The options page carries the extension's icon</strong> before its title. The guided tour's button now reads “Guided tour”, its full wording moving to the tooltip.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>Every label the toolkit ships is enabled by default</strong>, Conventional Comments' <code>typo</code>, <code>polish</code> and <code>quibble</code> included. A repository disables the ones it does not want.</li>
            <li><strong>If your configuration already defines <code>thanks</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>ack</code>, <code>disagree</code> or <code>reviewed</code>, it needs one property to keep working as before.</strong> Your label now merges into the shipped one and becomes always non-blocking, so <code>done (blocking):</code> is refused. Add <code>"alwaysNonBlocking": false</code> to restore it.</li>
            <li><strong>New icons:</strong> <code>praise</code> is now 👏, <code>decision</code> is ⚖️, and 🎉 goes to <code>reviewed</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>The check links to the toolkit's labels page by default</strong>, https://conventionalcomments.io/docs/labels/, instead of conventionalcomments.org, which documents none of the labels the toolkit adds. A <code>docUrl</code> you set yourself is kept.</li>
            <li><strong>Two active labels can no longer share a name.</strong> An alias that repeats another active label's name is removed, and the removal is reported. Before, the order of the label list decided which label a comment meant.</li>
            <li><strong>An enterprise floor no longer makes <code>decision</code> blocking.</strong> Listing it under <code>labels.minimum</code> used to force it blocking, so a bare <code>decision:</code> opened a blocking thread while <code>decision (blocking):</code> was refused. The floor now only keeps it enabled.</li>
            <li><strong>A shipped abbreviation only expands to an enabled label.</strong> <code>?ty</code>, <code>?po</code> and <code>?qui</code> used to insert the prefix of a disabled label, which validation then rejected. An abbreviation your repository defines itself is unaffected.</li>
            <li><strong>On Azure DevOps, the label prefix is no longer hidden and the subject no longer emphasised.</strong> Nobody has yet measured how Azure DevOps renders a comment body, and the extension stopped assuming it renders like GitHub's. Badges stay, and the body shows whole.</li>
            <li><strong>The companion server is retired, and no verifier ships in its place.</strong> It was never deployed. The specification now calls for a verifier that runs inside the platform and keeps no state, and <a href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/issues/56" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">issue #56</a> tracks building one for GitHub. Until it exists, nothing checks comments after the fact and no status check is published: <code>enforce</code> still blocks a send in the extension, which anyone without it bypasses. The <code>server</code> and <code>exemptionLog</code> configuration blocks are now ignored, with a warning.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>GitHub's “Files changed” view is recognised again.</strong> On pages as github.com serves them today, the extension found no button that publishes a comment, so under <code>enforce</code> a non-conforming comment could still be published there. It also treated editing a comment as writing a new one, treated the review summary as a line comment that can block the pull request, and gave no badges to threads. On the “Conversation” tab, editing a published comment was also treated as a line comment that can block. “Cancel” is still never intercepted.</li>
            <li><strong>GitHub: the pull request's creation date is read from the pull request itself.</strong> That date decides whether a pull request predates <code>activatedAt</code>, and so whether the extension may block on it. It used to be the first date on the page — on a merged pull request, the merge date, so the same pull request could read two dates depending on the tab. When the date cannot be read, the extension does not block.</li>
            <li><strong>The privacy policy described a history the extension does not keep.</strong> It announced “the last 50 times” an element was not recognised, while the journal keeps only one line per selection rule — fifty at most, each with its most recent observation. It now describes what is actually kept, the page address included.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-12">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.12</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 8, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.12" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The guided tour is legible at last — the page behind it really does go dark, and its card sits above it instead of blending in — and the “Status” section says what it observed rather than what that might mean.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The “Status” section tells you what it is for.</strong> It was the only section of the options page whose heading had nothing under it, so you had to read the two lines it shows to work out what it covered.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The guided tour now dims the page around the part it is pointing at.</strong> In the dark theme it barely dimmed at all — a dark veil over an already dark page changes almost nothing — so the tour looked like it was highlighting nothing, and its card, which had the same colour as the panels behind it, read as one more panel rather than as something floating above them. The rest of the page now goes properly dark in both themes, and the card carries a drop shadow you can actually see; in the dark theme it is also a lighter surface than the panels behind it.</li>
            <li><strong>The “Status” section no longer suggests the extension changed your pages.</strong> It reported “pages whose structure changed under it”, which reads as an accusation and, either way, claims more than the extension knows: what it records is that one of its selectors found nothing on the page. That can mean the page changed — or simply that the element is legitimately absent, like the merge button on a closed pull request. Both the section and the tour step now report what was actually observed.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-11">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.11</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 8, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.11" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">You now authorize every platform the same way — <code>github.com</code> included — so the extension asks before it works anywhere, and you can revoke that access at any time.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>You choose where the extension works, and you can change your mind.</strong> <code>github.com</code> used to be built into the extension: active from the moment you installed it, with no way to revoke that access from the extension itself. It now goes through the same one-click authorization as every other platform, and every domain you have allowed carries a “Withdraw” button. Domains authorized by your organisation's policy stay as they are — that is not this screen's call.</li>
            <li><strong>The options page opens automatically the first time and offers you a guided tour.</strong> It introduces each section and explains what it is for; you can skip it at any point, close it with <code>Escape</code>, and it never comes back on its own. A “Replay the guided tour” button in the page header replays it whenever you want.</li>
            <li><strong>The options page now speaks French or English, like the rest of the extension.</strong> It stayed in French whatever you had chosen — so someone who had set the interface to English saw English everywhere except on the settings page where they made that choice. The new language now takes effect immediately, rather than at the next reload.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>GitHub Enterprise Cloud with data residency now works at all.</strong> A domain allowed as <code>*.ghe.com</code> was recorded under a mangled name, so the extension never recognised the pages it was authorized for. This is older than this release, and no test could see it: the flaw only appears in a browser, and the tests ran where it does not.</li>
            <li><strong>You no longer need to reload the tab you came from.</strong> Allowing a domain from the options page left that tab inert — precisely the tab you were about to use. The extension now activates there immediately.</li>
            <li><strong>A failed revocation is no longer reported as successful.</strong> When the browser refuses to remove an access, the screen used to report the domain as unconfigured afterwards — an anomaly it had created itself, while the access was in fact still granted. It now changes nothing and says why.</li>
            <li><strong>Revoking access from <code>chrome://extensions</code> now also clears the saved platform.</strong> The platform you had chosen for that domain used to stay behind, so granting it again the same way silently restored the old choice instead of asking you.</li>
            <li><strong>The extension no longer loads itself on pages it does not support.</strong> If you had granted access to all sites from the browser's own controls, its code was injected into every <code>https</code> page, where it could do nothing anyway. Patterns that do not identify a concrete host no longer appear as domains to configure either.</li>
            <li><strong>The interface no longer appears twice on a page covered by two of your authorizations</strong>, and a tab left open for a long time no longer accumulates a copy of the extension's stylesheet.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>After this update, the extension is silent on <code>github.com</code> until you allow it.</strong> One click on “Activate” in the options page is enough. This step cannot be automated: the browser only grants a site permission after an explicit action from you. The page opens by itself to say so — but only if the update left you with no working domain at all; if you already had another one configured, the extension keeps working there and nothing tells you it is no longer active on <code>github.com</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>The options screen is rebuilt around the actual access states</strong>: domains the extension already knows, where one click both authorizes and classifies; any other domain, where you say which platform it belongs to before granting; the list of what is configured; and, only when it is not empty, the domains you allowed from the browser rather than from this screen, where the extension is not active yet.</li>
            <li><strong>Each <code>visualstudio.com</code> organisation is now authorized individually</strong> through the free-form field, with Azure DevOps pre-filled. The catalog entry it replaces asked for every organisation on those legacy addresses at once, when a workstation uses one.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-10">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.10</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 5, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.10" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">GitHub rewrote its “Files changed” view, and the extension had gone quiet on it: this release brings the whole toolkit back there, and makes diagnostics speak the language you picked.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The comment tooling works again on GitHub's rewritten “Files changed” view.</strong> GitHub now serves that page at <code>/pull/N/changes</code>, where the extension recognised nothing: no label toolbar, no quick entry, no live validation, no badges on published comments — precisely where a blocking <code>issue:</code> counts most. All of it is back. A page carrying an editing surface the extension does not recognise now leaves a diagnostic entry too, instead of being inert and silent at once.</li>
            <li><strong>Under <code>enforce</code>, a non-compliant comment can no longer be published from that view.</strong> Neither by clicking <em>Reply</em> under a diff line, nor from the “Finish your comments” panel of a batch review, whose buttons sit in the overlay rather than beside the field. Only the keyboard path was guarded before, so a comment the extension had flagged could still be sent with a mouse.</li>
            <li><strong>Editing the root comment of a thread is validated again there.</strong> It was taken for a reply, which the default configuration exempts from validation — an <code>issue:</code> root could be edited into anything without the extension looking. Published comments get their badges back too, resolved threads read as resolved instead of unknown, and the pull request banner shows the authors and bodies it was missing.</li>
            <li><strong>A blocking label in a review summary is warned about again.</strong> In the “Finish your comments” body, <code>issue:</code> reported everything except the one warning that matters there: nobody can resolve a review summary, so it carries no blocking state.</li>
            <li><strong>The Preview tab shows badges on that view too.</strong> Writing <code>issue: …</code> in a composer on <code>…/changes</code> and switching to <em>Preview</em> rendered the prefix in plain text, where the same switch on the conversation page showed the badge.</li>
            <li><strong>The composer looks like itself again on that view.</strong> The toolbar sits above the field instead of being folded into a narrow column beside it, and the compliance outline around the field is visible instead of clipped. That spacing survives a trip through the <em>Preview</em> tab as well.</li>
            <li><strong>Diagnostic messages now follow the language you picked in the options page.</strong> A French interface could show <em>Conforme, avec avertissements</em> above <em>This comment is blocking but has no discussion</em>: that one line took its language from the repository's configuration rather than from your own preference. Label names and diagnostic codes stay untranslated in both languages — they are identifiers.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-9">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.9</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 4, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.9" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The extension no longer gets in the way of a pull request's own description, no longer goes quiet on a page that starts out empty, and finally says why when it cannot read your configuration.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>When the configuration cannot be read, the options page now shows <strong>why</strong> — <code>HTTP 429</code>, a <code>TypeError</code>, a 404 that cannot be told from a refusal — together with the level it came from, instead of the bare word <code>unreachable</code>. The reason stays on your machine.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A pull request's own description is no longer treated as a review comment.</strong> The convention says nothing about it, yet the label toolbar was drawn on it — and under <code>enforce</code> the submit guard applied to its Update button, so a description without a <code>label:</code> prefix could not be saved at all.</li>
            <li><strong>The “Configuration unread” banner no longer appears on every repository.</strong> Every configuration read was failing before it left the browser, so the extension always fell back to its degraded state.</li>
            <li><strong>A page that had nothing to show no longer stops watching after five seconds.</strong> On <em>Files changed</em> with no thread yet, the first comment posted afterwards got neither badge nor banner until a full reload.</li>
            <li><strong>A missing page is no longer read as a hidden one while you are signed in.</strong> Two independent signals must now agree before a 404 means an unreadable configuration.</li>
            <li><strong>The selector degradation journal is usable again.</strong> Visiting a single closed pull request filled all fifty lines with one entry and evicted every real degradation.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-8">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.8</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 4, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.8" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Published comments read like titles — badges, then the subject in bold on the same line — and three ways a comment could silently lose its badges are closed.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>A published comment's subject is now shown <strong>in bold on the badge line</strong>, to the right of the badges. Where the subject cannot be delimited safely, the previous layout is kept rather than risking a wrong cut.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>Editing a comment no longer strips its badges. The platform rewrites the rendered body on an update, and nothing brought them back until you reloaded.</li>
            <li>The structured prefix is re-hidden when the platform restores it on its own.</li>
            <li>Leaving a pull request, or switching to <code>mode: off</code>, no longer leaves part of a comment invisible — an extension that declares itself inactive went on concealing text you had written.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li>The composer toolbar now stacks <strong>two rows</strong> — labels, then decorations — so a narrow window can no longer put a label button next to a decoration segment as if they were one kind of control.</li>
            <li>Label buttons no longer show their icon; badges on published comments still do. The buttons share one row's width, where an icon costs every other button.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-7">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.7</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 3, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.7" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The structured prefix disappears from a published comment — the badges already say it — except everywhere saying it twice was the safer answer.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><code>issue (blocking):</code> no longer appears in a published comment. On screen only — the stored comment is untouched, and reopening the edit form shows it in full.</li>
            <li>Where the badges would not say the same thing, the text stays: a decoration the configuration rejects, a case defect the tool exists to flag, decorations folded into a <code>+N</code> badge.</li>
            <li>A line that merely looks like a prefix — in a code block, a quote, a list, a heading, a table — is left alone.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li>Every default label now has an icon and a colour, contrast-checked against WCAG 1.4.11.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-6">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.6</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 3, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.6" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Decorations get badges of their own, and the whole display follows a configuration change while you watch.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>Each decoration now gets its own badge beside the label: red where it forces blocking, green where it forces non-blocking, dashed where the decoration is declared nowhere.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>Changing the configuration now refreshes what is already on screen, including on a tab left open on a pull request.</li>
            <li><strong>A comment box you already had open follows that change too</strong>, including whether it blocks your submission — an organisation switching from <code>enforce</code> to <code>off</code> left you blocked until you reopened the box.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-5">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.5</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 2, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.5" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Repository configuration is readable again on private repositories.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>The read follows a redirect the browser refuses to authenticate; credentials are now dropped only on that hop, so the file stays readable without asking you for any host permission.</li>
            <li>A configuration file unreadable for want of a session is no longer mistaken for a missing one — which silently applied the wrong configuration.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-4">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.4</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 1, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.4" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The decoration selector says what your comment carries, not what you last clicked.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>A decoration you removed by editing the text, or one that left with a label the configuration disabled, no longer stays lit in the toolbar.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-3">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.3</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 1, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.3" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Two permissions dropped from the manifest, telemetry that exists only if you ask for it, and enough to submit to the stores.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong><code>activeTab</code> and the permanent host permission on <code>github.com</code> are gone.</strong> Hosts are granted on demand — the extension asks for what it needs, when it needs it.</li>
            <li>The pull request banner is a summary, not a merge barrier. The verdict belongs to the platform's check, already red and already blocking; replaying it here devalued both.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>Optional telemetry, off until you turn it on, bound to the address you consented to and configured by your organization's policy rather than by a repository file.</li>
            <li>The version shown in <code>chrome://extensions</code> now distinguishes pre-releases.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>On GitHub Enterprise Server and self-hosted Azure DevOps Server, granting the host permission finally gives you the extension.</strong> The platform each host serves is now recorded with it.</li>
            <li><strong>An organization configuration hosted off your platform is readable again.</strong> It never had been: the page's CORS policy blocked it whatever permission was granted.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-2">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.2</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">August 24, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.2" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Bot and tool comments stop being flagged, and the toolbar stops touching the edge.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A tool command is no longer flagged as a malformed comment.</strong> A slash command, or a bot mention you list in <code>toolCommands</code>, is exempt — <code>/rebase</code> and <code>@dependabot recreate</code> pass through intact.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>The toolbar and the feedback align with the comment box's text instead of touching its border.</li>
            <li>A label's colour now paints the active button's border rather than its background: that colour comes from your configuration, and using it as a fill dropped contrast to 1.54 where 4.5 is required.</li>
            <li>Toolbar buttons are spaced again — a CSS comment closed one character early was silently swallowing the rule that spaced them.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

